
  [1mB. Observable session store[0m — everything in A, plus [38;5;153msubscribe(fn)[39m that fires on local writes [3mand[0m on the [38;5;153mwindow[39m [38;5;153mstorage
[39m  event, which browsers dispatch to other tabs when [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m changes. Log out in one tab and every other tab
  reacts.
  [3mTrades:[0m solves exactly the staleness hole I flagged when recommending [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m, at the cost of subscription
  lifecycle (unsubscribe, avoiding leaks) for zero subscribers today. [3mComplexity: moderate.[0m

  [1mC. [38;5;153mlogin()[39m owns the write[0m — [38;5;153mlogin()[39m calls into the session module itself, so the caller just awaits it and the store
  is populated as a side effect.
  [3mTrades:[0m fewest steps at the call site, but it welds auth transport to browser storage. [38;5;153mlogin()[39m stops being testable
  without a DOM, and a function whose name promises authentication silently mutating persistent state is the kind of
  hidden coupling that gets hard to unpick once three forms depend on it. [3mComplexity: trivial.[0m I'd rule this one out.

  [1mMy recommendation: A, with B's [38;5;153msubscribe[39m deliberately deferred.[0m The module boundary is the part that's expensive to
  get wrong and it's identical in both; adding [38;5;153msubscribe()[39m later is purely additive and breaks no existing consumer.
  Building pub/sub now means maintaining a notification system with nothing subscribed to it. B wins the moment you have
  a second long-lived view that must react without a reload — at which point it's a small addition to the same file,
  not a redesign.

  Keeping the write at the call site (A/B over C) also means [38;5;153mlogin()[39m remains a plain function you can test without a
  browser, which matters more once it stops being a stub.
